    proof: "Given a basis v₁, …, vₙ, define φᵢ on the basis by the displayed rule and extend linearly. Such a linear functional exists and is unique because a linear map is determined by its values on a basis. The φᵢ are independent: if ∑ aᵢ φᵢ = 0, apply both sides to vⱼ to get aⱼ = 0. They span the dual space. Any linear functional φ agrees with ∑ⱼ φ(vⱼ) φⱼ on every basis vector, because the right side sends vₖ to φ(vₖ). Two linear functionals that agree on a basis are equal. So the dual basis exists, and the displayed equation is the relation that defines it.",
